1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,160
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,680
We're going hiking next Saturday.

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:10,960
Now take this note to your mother and get it signed.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,060
And we got the note signed.

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,780
Went down to the Army-Navy store, bought it out.

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,400
Had picks, axes, shovels, everything.

7
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:26,900
And I had all this stuff, man, which any guy, normal guy, going to war would have.

8
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:29,280
You know, except for the gun and the bazooka.

9
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:33,400
Got up 4.30 in the morning before the crack of dawn.

10
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:36,560
Loaded down with cans of beans and matches.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:38,720
You know, the blue tip Ohio matches.

12
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,340
I had a little manual.

13
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:46,840
I'm all ready to dig a hole and put the logs down in the fire and take them beans on the skillet and do it upright.

14
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,440
Had USN forks and knives and everything.

15
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:51,580
We were really cool, man.

16
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:54,420
And everybody's all dressed with the packs on the back.

17
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:57,480
And we bought brogues, you know, heavy marching shoes.

18
00:00:57,540 --> 00:00:58,660
We were ready to go on a hike.

19
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:00,900
Went right out there and caught a trolley car.

20
00:01:02,639 --> 00:01:06,679
Hey, that's Bill Cosby talking about being a Boy Scout in the beautiful city of Philadelphia.

21
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,679
Hopping on the trolley and hiking in the wilds of Fairmount Park.

22
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,179
Not real wild, but a great little story.

23
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,140
Hey, this is podcast number six.

24
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:24,380
Hey.

25
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:25,120
Hey.

26
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:50,720
Hi, this is Clark Green. Welcome back to the podcast.

27
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,960
I want to tell you about a web resource that I think you'll enjoy.

28
00:01:53,960 --> 00:02:03,339
It's called theinquiry.net, and I'll have a link to it on the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

29
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:10,979
It is a huge historical resource put together by Rick Seymour, and he has some great advice

30
00:02:10,979 --> 00:02:19,920
and also kind of an endless number of program ideas and outlines and things of interest to scouts.

31
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:27,420
And Rick features old school scouting, so look for lots of wood skills and things like that.

32
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:34,700
It's just an amazing piece of work, and congratulations, Rick, on putting together something that has been very valuable to me personally,

33
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:38,859
and I hope we can spread the word a little bit with the podcast.

34
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,799
Pretty full slate today. Let's see.

35
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:49,380
In the Scoutmaster ship from seven minutes, I'm going to read you an essay from Andy, the Net Commissioner at Ask Andy.

36
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:53,900
Then talk a little bit about something we call the moccasin telegraph.

37
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:57,120
If you don't know about the moccasin telegraph, well, we'll get you informed.

38
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:06,000
Then we're going to have part one of a feature about moving your troop from being adult-led to youth-led.

39
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,960
I hope you'll find it valuable. We're going to do that over the next several podcasts, have little installments,

40
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:14,940
and then we'll end up with a Scoutmaster's Minute, so let's get ourselves moving.

41
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:20,380
Scoutmaster ship in seven minutes or less.

42
00:03:20,380 --> 00:03:39,420
Andy is a commissioner, and he has an online column where he answers questions that people send in about various scouting conundrums,

43
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:41,720
and every once in a while he offers a little essay.

44
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:46,640
I really love this essay, and I think it's an important thing for everybody

45
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:50,899
because it's a good debunking of that word, Scoutmaster.

46
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,559
He says this,

47
00:03:52,559 --> 00:03:59,160
If you're a regular reader of my column, you already know about tyrants and tin gods, renegades and recalcitrants,

48
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:05,660
bullies and belligerents, dictators, martinettes, and the world's oldest patrol leaders masquerading as Scoutmasters.

49
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:12,140
And thank goodness that for every one of them, there are a thousand more dedicated scouting leaders who get it right.

50
00:04:13,119 --> 00:04:14,200
But how did this happen?

51
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:18,060
How did we get so far away from true north in these cases?

52
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:24,760
Aside from warped mentalities seeking out this position in order to foment their brand of meanness,

53
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,200
is there some sort of cultural error or acquiescence that has abetted this?

54
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:31,000
I think there might be.

55
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:38,219
A hundred years ago, while Baden-Powell was formulating how the Scout movement would be structured and organized,

56
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,640
and what nomenclature would be used,

57
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,760
one of the decisions he needed to make was this.

58
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:51,300
What designation do we use in referring to the adult volunteer who guides and mentors the boys and young men in the troop?

59
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:56,280
Since he considered scouting for boys largely a system of education,

60
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:04,460
it was a natural and small step for him to borrow from the educational system used in Britain at the time.

61
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:09,820
This would aid in understanding the role of that adult volunteer.

62
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:16,380
So, to parallel the role of the male teacher who was commonly referred to as a schoolmaster,

63
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:20,580
the very natural choice was scoutmaster.

64
00:05:21,679 --> 00:05:25,359
Its meaning in the time and country of war origin was straightforward.

65
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:29,620
Scoutmaster equals a teacher of scouts.

66
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,980
When the Boy Scout movement crossed the pond to America,

67
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:36,800
some things remained intact and others changed, including the scout oath and law,

68
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,640
but the term scoutmaster remained even though in America there was no educational equivalent.

69
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:47,120
Teachers in America were and still are called teachers, not schoolmasters.

70
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:54,700
The decision to use scoutmaster instead of a designation appropriate to American culture proved unfortunate

71
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:59,060
because scoutmaster was not instantly translated into teacher of scouts.

72
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:03,620
Instead, it was translated as master of scouts.

73
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:10,180
And to Americans, master meant, and still means, the one in charge, the boss, and so on.

74
00:06:10,620 --> 00:06:13,980
The true meaning became lost in the translation.

75
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:20,560
Even Webster's, when they define scoutmaster, gets it not quite right.

76
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:28,700
The adult leader in charge of a troop of Boy Scouts is what Webster defines scoutmaster as.

77
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:36,000
In fact, the United States is the only English-speaking country in which the now-anachronistic scoutmaster remains.

78
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:43,740
The Scout Association in the United Kingdom and Scouts Australia have both for many years referred to the position

79
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:45,260
as scout leader.

80
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:49,080
In Canada, it's a scouter, or a troop scouter.

81
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:54,500
On top of this, two movies very popular in their times reinforce the notion that

82
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:58,440
the adult is in charge of the troop and direct leader of the boys in it.

83
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:01,000
In effect, the adult patrol leader.

84
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:05,900
The first, in 1953, was Mr. Scoutmaster, starring Clifton Webb,

85
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,660
as kind of Mr. Belvedere, fuss-budget personality type.

86
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:17,920
The second was produced in 1966 and is currently enjoying a kind of a resurgence, at least among scouters.

87
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:21,320
It's Follow Me Boys, starring Fred McMurray.

88
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:26,580
In this latter film, someone did pay attention to accuracy,

89
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:30,060
but the non-initiated will still come away with the impression

90
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:34,620
that the scoutmaster is the day-to-day leader of a troop of Boy Scouts.

91
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:39,400
On top of these, we had the popular one-panel comic,

92
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:42,180
The Little Scouts, by cartoonist Ronald Coe,

93
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:47,640
featuring a rotund and clueless scoutmaster with his bunch of Little Scouts.

94
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:51,600
This comic thrived in the 1930s and through most of the 40s,

95
00:07:52,220 --> 00:07:56,740
and although it did much to popularize Boy Scouting,

96
00:07:57,080 --> 00:08:01,380
it did little to dissuade folks from the notion that Boy Scouts are led by adults.

97
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:07,860
Speaking of comics, we can't overlook Charles M. Schultz's Snoopy of Peanuts fame,

98
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:12,640
who became the Beagle Scout leader of Woodstock and his little friends way back in 1974.

99
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:17,560
Again, a depiction of one large leader and a bunch of little followers.

100
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:22,440
Bottom line, just about everything in American culture that's referenced scouting

101
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:27,200
has done so by depicting the scoutmaster as a master of scouts.

102
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:32,659
Is it any wonder that we seem to perpetuate this fiction in troop upon troop around the country?

103
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:33,840
So, what to do?

104
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,220
Let's begin, first of all, by throwing the rascals out.

105
00:08:37,559 --> 00:08:39,360
Got a scoutmaster that just doesn't get it?

106
00:08:39,559 --> 00:08:40,280
Get rid of him.

107
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:42,500
Oh, you're worried about hurting his feelings?

108
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:47,340
Just stop and consider how he's hurting the lives of boys and young men, sometimes permanently.

109
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:50,060
If that doesn't tell you that he needs to be done,

110
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:53,500
no words of mine are likely to help you grow a spine and do what's right.

111
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:55,000
Worried about who will take his place?

112
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,340
Maybe by taking action, another parent will realize somebody has a spine,

113
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:02,160
and on that basis, step up and be counted too.

114
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:08,920
Second, let's from now on put foremost in our minds that scoutmaster doesn't mean master of anything.

115
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:14,020
In fact, if we substitute servant for master, we'd be a lot closer to the truth of the matter.

116
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:17,960
Even better, let's substitute teacher or mentor or guide.

117
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:21,240
This is what scoutmastering is all about.

118
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:25,620
As Baden-Powell himself put it, it's being an older brother to the boy.

119
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,300
Find the best in the boy and bring it out.

120
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:30,100
So that's Andy's little essay.

121
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,580
And I've got to tell you, I think he hit the nail right on the head.

122
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:35,700
Now, his advice to dump him seems pretty harsh.

123
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:38,240
But I want you to consider the source.

124
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:41,500
Andy has a lot of years of experience as a commissioner.

125
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:45,420
And was a scout as a boy and an Eagle Scout.

126
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:47,700
You know, very experienced fellow.

127
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,300
Commissioners are there to sort out problems.

128
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:55,500
The first thing they learn by hard experience is that problem people don't change.

129
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:56,840
They really have to go.

130
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:01,220
They can't be trained or treated or threatened or cajoled into changing.

131
00:10:01,460 --> 00:10:04,380
They simply have to step aside or be dumped.

132
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:06,180
And that's a sad but true fact.

133
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:10,380
Most scoutmasters are valuable, wonderful people who do the best they can.

134
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:15,479
And it takes an experienced eye in about three minutes to tell the difference between troops

135
00:10:15,479 --> 00:10:19,739
that are functioning well and ones that are in trouble because they have somebody who

136
00:10:19,739 --> 00:10:21,959
misapprehends the term scoutmaster.

137
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:23,239
Just a little food for thought.

138
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:26,880
I think, like I said, Andy really hit the nail on the head with that one.

139
00:10:27,319 --> 00:10:29,439
Do check him out at Ask Andy, his online column.

140
00:10:29,440 --> 00:10:38,640
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

141
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:47,740
So when the troops at summer camp every evening, the patrols will sit down and do entries in

142
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:48,600
their patrol log.

143
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:53,960
And then after that's done, we'll have the patrol leaders get together with the patrol leaders

144
00:10:53,960 --> 00:11:00,180
council and kind of, you know, do a little reflection on the day and see what's going on.

145
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:06,920
And it's always a good time for the adult leaders to kind of catch up and see what the scouts have

146
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:11,200
been up to all day, handle any little difficulties or problems that may have arisen.

147
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:18,960
And one time this past summer was particularly memorable.

148
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:19,300
Thank you.

149
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:36,640
One of the patrol leaders mentioned that as an entry in their patrol log that one of the

150
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:43,300
fellows in the patrol was really kind of uncomfortable with the snake that was apparently had taken

151
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:45,180
up residence under his tent platform.

152
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:52,640
Now we use, you know, wooden tent platforms and pitch wall tents on them.

153
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:55,839
And the scouts stay in those throughout the week.

154
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:03,640
And this kind of caught my attention because I've mentioned before, we have one very interesting

155
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:08,219
species of snake at our camp, the copperhead.

156
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:15,200
So after the patrol leaders council dismissed, very quietly took another adult leader and

157
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:16,560
we went to visit that tent platform.

158
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:30,860
Well, sure enough, it was the snake I suspected, a copperhead, which is a poisonous snake.

159
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:38,560
So while the scouts were off to the showers, we kind of disassembled the tent platform and

160
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:47,900
sent for the nature fellows who have the prerequisite equipment to extract a snake from the tent platform.

161
00:12:48,580 --> 00:12:53,120
And we noodled around for a while and finally we got the thing and then we took it way out

162
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:54,560
in the woods and released it.

163
00:12:57,760 --> 00:13:02,960
And the scout was no longer uncomfortable because there was a snake underneath of that.

164
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:20,940
So fast forward to the next day and one of my assistants is walking down the trail towards

165
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:24,760
our campsite and ahead of him are three or four scouts from another troop.

166
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:31,560
And he was listening to them relate the story of this giant poisonous snake that was found

167
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:38,520
in one of the campsites last night and that the scoutmaster had to kill it and, you know,

168
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:44,700
had to rescue several young scouts from its grasp of its harbor jobs.

169
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:59,840
And apparently had skinned it and made it into a hat band.

170
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:03,620
You know what we call that at our camp?

171
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:05,760
We call it the moccasin telegram.

172
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:21,360
We call it the moccasin telegram.

173
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:28,680
And then we, as someone who cracks tờ, Petitie will help them.

174
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:30,860
Treat Susanne Framinski on iTunes.

175
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:43,260
For the next several podcasts, we're going to be discussing how do you change your troop from being an adult-led enterprise to a scout-led enterprise.

176
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:51,980
It's not as difficult as you may think, and mostly what you have to deal with are just basically a few unfounded fears on the part of adults.

177
00:14:52,220 --> 00:15:00,700
Placing the leadership of a scout troop in the hands of the scouts, getting a troop into the habit of youth leadership, it's an excellent piece of service that you can do.

178
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:09,440
But before all this can happen, the adult leadership has to understand exactly what they're trying to accomplish, and they need to define their relationship to the scouts,

179
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:16,040
and they need to overcome any reservations they might have with investing the scouts with actual responsibility and leadership.

180
00:15:16,520 --> 00:15:29,840
I think the single most important concept for us all as adult leaders to really get and to maintain, and one that I mention very often, is that a scout troop belongs to scouts.

181
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:32,480
It does not belong to adults.

182
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:39,480
The adults are there to facilitate, to administer things, but not to run the troop.

183
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:43,880
Scouting is not a program that we prepare and present for the scouts.

184
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:48,160
It's not a program that we do in cooperation with the scouts.

185
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:51,480
I would even go so far as to say that.

186
00:15:51,620 --> 00:15:54,460
It is a program that scouts do for themselves.

187
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:59,800
It's not a curriculum or a set of object lessons or useful skills.

188
00:16:00,640 --> 00:16:09,560
It is an experiential program that scouts do for themselves in self-motivation, self-government, self-realization.

189
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:13,440
Adult leaders aren't chief executive officers.

190
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:15,600
They're not the captain of the team.

191
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:17,400
They're not the sergeant of the platoon.

192
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:24,220
They are coaches and mentors to the actual leaders of a scout troop, and that's the scouts.

193
00:16:26,199 --> 00:16:29,819
Now, coaches and mentors, they help develop skills.

194
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:31,260
They provide motivation.

195
00:16:31,540 --> 00:16:32,620
They provide inspiration.

196
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:37,040
But they can only observe the game being played from the sidelines.

197
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:43,160
Two common fears keep a lot of troops in the hands of adults.

198
00:16:43,319 --> 00:16:49,740
First of all, we fear that given broad decision-making capacities, scouts will make bad decisions.

199
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:53,339
And I am here to tell you that they will occasionally make a bad decision.

200
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:56,120
But we're going to be training and mentoring them.

201
00:16:56,500 --> 00:17:01,980
We're going to encourage them to find the proper approach and to learn from their mistakes.

202
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:04,599
And we're going to show them how to get things back on track.

203
00:17:04,599 --> 00:17:07,520
We're going to review things and talk about them.

204
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:17,540
We're going to work hard on making decent leaders out of the scouts, wise, mature leaders who make good decisions.

205
00:17:18,599 --> 00:17:29,800
The other main thing I think that people fear is that the scouts, if they are given the keys to the asylum, are going to just have the wrong emphasis about everything.

206
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:34,199
I mean, why would they do any of the more or less difficult or challenging things in scouting?

207
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:36,879
If they can just make a decision not to do them.

208
00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:43,080
Why go on camping trips if we can just maybe have pizza parties?

209
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:49,100
Why go to a backpacking trip if we can just go to an amusement park?

210
00:17:49,679 --> 00:17:51,439
Well, the answer for that is simple.

211
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:54,280
Boys don't have to be forced into scouting.

212
00:17:54,399 --> 00:17:55,959
They are doing it voluntarily.

213
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:58,840
And they're doing it because it's something that appeals to them.

214
00:17:58,879 --> 00:18:00,480
They want to go camping and backpacking.

215
00:18:00,540 --> 00:18:02,939
They want to do the challenging and difficult things.

216
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:05,020
That's what they like about it.

217
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:10,820
They might kill a few of the leader's sacred cows that really don't amount to a hill of beans.

218
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:12,580
How's that for a mixed metaphor?

219
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:20,880
Now, once we've settled on the idea that this is something that we want to do and that we should not be afraid of doing,

220
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:32,620
and we understand that we're going to help youth develop into mature, intelligent leaders who make decent decisions and who know their program,

221
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,640
we have to think about what really matters.

222
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,600
Remember the sacred cows and the hills of beans?

223
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:43,960
Advancement in ranks and earning merit badges and making eagle all recognize something about scouts.

224
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:47,580
I mean, it means that they've passed through a number of challenges and experiences,

225
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:52,580
and as laudable as they are, the results are not really the end in themselves.

226
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:56,880
Scouting is more about the process that leads to the results.

227
00:18:57,940 --> 00:19:01,700
Scouts will learn some useful skills, but they're not the whole story, are they?

228
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:08,000
What they take away from the experience is confident self-knowledge, the ability to work well with other people.

229
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:16,160
So, I mean, focus on creating and preserving an atmosphere where scouts can have those experiences and forget about the rest of the stuff.

230
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:17,660
And I'm serious about that.

231
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:18,880
Forget about it.

232
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:24,100
Listen, you're going to fear, as I did, that the scouts won't do a very good job of things.

233
00:19:24,460 --> 00:19:29,880
And if they don't do a very good job of things and they do things poorly, that that's going to let down all the other scouts,

234
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:34,600
and so I should really step in and do things better, and you get into this little circle of responsibility.

235
00:19:34,600 --> 00:19:39,480
And you think, well, I can't let this troop suffer under bad leadership.

236
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,899
I need to step in and I need to fix everything.

237
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,919
And that can really sabotage the entire enterprise.

238
00:19:47,620 --> 00:19:55,360
I mean, I've watched troop meetings that, in my humble opinion, were really poorly planned, poorly presented,

239
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:58,959
ones that adults certainly could have done much better.

240
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:08,060
I've worried that my scouts were not prepared properly for outings, and I wondered if we should step in and maybe prepare them better.

241
00:20:08,060 --> 00:20:17,360
I have learned that even if scouts have conducted what I may see as a lackluster troop meeting and planned what I may consider ill-conceived outings,

242
00:20:17,560 --> 00:20:23,260
what is really important is that they have done this under their own leadership.

243
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:29,540
They're doing and learning things that their peers outside of scouting aren't.

244
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:36,780
And my direct responsibility as a scoutmaster is not the quality of the program.

245
00:20:37,320 --> 00:20:42,160
It's not the number of advancements or the number of scouts that we have advancing,

246
00:20:42,520 --> 00:20:48,140
or any of the other, you know, 700 numbers that we are habitually paying attention to.

247
00:20:48,140 --> 00:20:57,480
But our real job is to inspire youth leadership towards achieving the real goals of scouting.

248
00:20:57,880 --> 00:21:00,740
And that is really the only thing that matters.

249
00:21:00,740 --> 00:21:13,440
Boy Scouts!

250
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:17,320
Boy Scouts!

251
00:21:28,480 --> 00:21:29,040
Boy Scouts!

252
00:21:29,040 --> 00:21:29,960
That's right, it's time.

253
00:21:30,740 --> 00:21:32,480
For a Scoutmaster's Minute.

254
00:21:40,480 --> 00:21:44,700
A scout is cheerful. This one's easy.

255
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:47,540
Of course a scout is cheerful.

256
00:21:47,920 --> 00:21:51,400
Most boys the age of scouts, they're always cheerful.

257
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:57,440
Yeah, okay, when they become teenagers, yeah, I gotcha.

258
00:21:57,440 --> 00:22:00,500
But a scout looks on the bright side of life.

259
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:06,240
He cheerfully does the things that come in his way, and he tries to make other people cheerful too.

260
00:22:06,680 --> 00:22:07,300
Watch them.

261
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:09,120
Observe, my friend.

262
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:11,520
And you will see that this is what they usually are.

263
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:14,760
It's the natural state of most boys.

264
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:17,280
They could be sad and tired and frustrated or afraid.

265
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:24,940
But given the opportunity and a little bit of time, they resume that cheerful demeanor pretty quickly.

266
00:22:26,080 --> 00:22:28,780
Cheerfulness comes from a lot of opportunities.

267
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:33,520
Opportunity to challenge themselves rather than having a challenge thrust on them.

268
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:35,980
Letting them choose.

269
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:37,879
Letting them choose some of these things.

270
00:22:37,980 --> 00:22:42,740
Opportunity to explore and discover this incredible world of ours.

271
00:22:43,180 --> 00:22:47,500
To form and cement friendships with one another.

272
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:54,060
To experience nature in that wonderful, glorious outdoor world.

273
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:57,419
And the opportunity to sit and think.

274
00:22:57,820 --> 00:23:01,419
To work, to play, and sometimes just to sit.

275
00:23:03,340 --> 00:23:04,940
There's enough demands on our time.

276
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:07,760
We don't need to have every minute of our day program, do we?

277
00:23:08,159 --> 00:23:09,500
Sometimes we just want to sit.

278
00:23:11,220 --> 00:23:15,980
Most of the opportunities will arise naturally in the course of scouting.

279
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:19,179
You know, we go camping.

280
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:19,980
We have meetings.

281
00:23:20,139 --> 00:23:20,820
We do things.

282
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:23,600
Most of these opportunities are going to arise naturally.

283
00:23:23,600 --> 00:23:28,500
What else we have to do as scout leaders is make sure that nobody stands in the way of those opportunities.

284
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:32,179
And our guys are going to be cheerful.

285
00:23:32,719 --> 00:23:37,060
Because A, it's the natural state of most boys.

286
00:23:37,659 --> 00:23:39,659
And B, a scout is cheerful.

287
